[{:db/id 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-71a6807f-51a3-5e94-a65a-0087aa5dc119" "question-e5a0af42-71ea-5bb3-8eb8-b8b29ef8a2c6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166504 :question/problem "If ![](images/00/0009074502b2f719bcc6c4ba4726e93a3c75386be4e7f9c19a8a33336f8c7fba.svg) and ![](images/87/8774988415a7fdb8bd16aa2bd2ccd03a7e94ffd272b23a11bc2f561d855f2e8a.svg) are solutions to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ find $A\\mathbf{w}(t)$ where ![](images/57/5712d4375732622b9bd533e98d61ba41e0057c88b9daf0e48d12ef9c9465e3d8.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-71a6807f-51a3-5e94-a65a-0087aa5dc119" :question/id #uuid "71a6807f-51a3-5e94-a65a-0087aa5dc119" :question/math-academy-id "q-330333" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)\\times {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier is not a constant.\n\n- Option III is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=0$ and ${c}_{2}=-3,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6f7e02fc-389b-5a53-ab92-9e7d664ea877"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6f7e02fc-389b-5a53-ab92-9e7d664ea877" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f7e02fc-389b-5a53-ab92-9e7d664ea877" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d51c3cc8-7840-5e1e-8a53-1bf6714d0841" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e8a1cc24-066e-5fd9-aec3-63dcebd27b5a" "answer-cd9aacf5-3935-547f-8a01-96b4e4f8e273" "answer-892c08a4-3464-5326-833d-0cac32b879f0" "answer-d51c3cc8-7840-5e1e-8a53-1bf6714d0841" "answer-8cfbf6a0-2987-5862-8e6d-5405fa26152f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8a1cc24-066e-5fd9-aec3-63dcebd27b5a" :answer/id #uuid "e8a1cc24-066e-5fd9-aec3-63dcebd27b5a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd9aacf5-3935-547f-8a01-96b4e4f8e273" :answer/id #uuid "cd9aacf5-3935-547f-8a01-96b4e4f8e273" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-892c08a4-3464-5326-833d-0cac32b879f0" :answer/id #uuid "892c08a4-3464-5326-833d-0cac32b879f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d51c3cc8-7840-5e1e-8a53-1bf6714d0841" :answer/id #uuid "d51c3cc8-7840-5e1e-8a53-1bf6714d0841" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cfbf6a0-2987-5862-8e6d-5405fa26152f" :answer/id #uuid "8cfbf6a0-2987-5862-8e6d-5405fa26152f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e5a0af42-71ea-5bb3-8eb8-b8b29ef8a2c6" :question/id #uuid "e5a0af42-71ea-5bb3-8eb8-b8b29ef8a2c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-330354" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multipliers of the first and the second terms are not constant.\n\n- Option III is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1f03c56a-d1c9-5e79-89bf-0c51f2598a8f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1f03c56a-d1c9-5e79-89bf-0c51f2598a8f" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f03c56a-d1c9-5e79-89bf-0c51f2598a8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cf25f748-5f77-5753-848b-d0fd49da67dc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cf25f748-5f77-5753-848b-d0fd49da67dc" "answer-eae566d9-a61e-5385-975a-ff789b6dc62b" "answer-e0c4b1ce-27b0-544b-a5c2-9c96a3531721" "answer-b96cdf9a-49a4-54e5-9dfc-848510bf8222" "answer-072d3215-6073-5918-a9fe-1dcc32903f2d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf25f748-5f77-5753-848b-d0fd49da67dc" :answer/id #uuid "cf25f748-5f77-5753-848b-d0fd49da67dc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eae566d9-a61e-5385-975a-ff789b6dc62b" :answer/id #uuid "eae566d9-a61e-5385-975a-ff789b6dc62b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0c4b1ce-27b0-544b-a5c2-9c96a3531721" :answer/id #uuid "e0c4b1ce-27b0-544b-a5c2-9c96a3531721" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b96cdf9a-49a4-54e5-9dfc-848510bf8222" :answer/id #uuid "b96cdf9a-49a4-54e5-9dfc-848510bf8222" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-072d3215-6073-5918-a9fe-1dcc32903f2d" :answer/id #uuid "072d3215-6073-5918-a9fe-1dcc32903f2d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167977 :tutorial/content "Given two solutions to a linear system of differential equations, any *constant-coefficient* linear combination of those solutions is also a solution to the system. This property of linear systems is called the **linearity principle** (or the **superposition principle**).\n\nLet's state this again, more precisely. If $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{v}(t)$ are solutions to the homogeneous matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \nthen the expression \n\n$$\nc \\, \\mathbf{u}(t) + d \\, \\mathbf{v}(t)\n$$\n\n \nis also a solution to that equation for all $c,d\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nWe can prove this by substituting $\\mathbf x(t) = c\\mathbf{u}(t) +d\\mathbf{v}(t)$ into the matrix differential equation and verifying that it results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t) \\\\ (c\\,u(t)+d\\,v(t))^{′} & =A(c\\,u(t)+d\\,v(t)) \\\\ c\\,{u}^{′}(t)+d\\,{v}^{′}(t) & =cA\\,u(t)+dA\\,v(t) \\\\ c\\,{u}^{′}(t)+d\\,{v}^{′}(t) & =c\\,{u}^{′}(t)+d\\,{v}^{′}(t)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $c\\,\\mathbf{u}(t) +d\\,\\mathbf{v}(t)$ is a solution!\n\nIn general, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations states that if \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_{2}(t), \\: \\ldots, \\: \\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nare solutions to a homogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\nc_1 \\, \\mathbf{x}_{1}(t) + c_2 \\, \\mathbf{x}_{2}(t) + \\cdots + c_k \\, \\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith $c_1, c_2, \\ldots, c_k \\in\\mathbb R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system.\n\nNext, let's see a concrete example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167008 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
